steps.body:
Valuing a position needs a mid, and a book's value needs every position's. With coroutines that's `const std::int64_t m = co_await mid(bid, ask);`, one coroutine awaiting another's result. The type that makes it work is a **Task**. It's lazy, since its `initial_suspend` returns `std::suspend_always` and nothing runs until someone awaits it, and it's its own awaiter:

- `await_suspend(awaiter)` stores the awaiting coroutine's handle in the task's promise as its **continuation**, then returns the task's own handle, so the task starts now.
- When the task finishes, `final_suspend` returns an awaiter whose `await_suspend` returns the continuation, so the awaiting coroutine resumes, and `await_resume` hands it the result.

Returning a handle from `await_suspend` is **symmetric transfer**: the compiler resumes that coroutine with a jump, a tail call that reuses the current stack frame, instead of a call nested inside this one. The obvious version calls `resume()` from inside `final_suspend`'s awaiter:

```cpp norun
// nested: each resume runs inside the call before it, and the stack grows
void await_suspend(std::coroutine_handle<promise_type> me) noexcept { me.promise().continuation.resume(); }

// symmetric transfer: return the next coroutine, and the compiler jumps to it
std::coroutine_handle<> await_suspend(std::coroutine_handle<promise_type> me) noexcept {
    return me.promise().continuation;
}
```

When a loop awaits a million tasks that each finish at once, every nested resume runs inside the one before, and the stack overflows. Built that way, the example's million awaits crashed here with a segmentation fault, exit status 139.

Symmetric transfer depends on the compiler making the jump. Apple clang makes it at any optimization level. g++ 10.2 makes it only at `-O2`, `-O3`, and `-Os`, the levels that turn tail calls into jumps: built at `-O0`, the example crashed between 60,000 and 70,000 awaits, and at `-O1` before a million, so its first line is `// -O2 -std=c++20 -fcoroutines`. `std::noop_coroutine()` returns a handle that does nothing when resumed, the continuation for a task that `main` starts itself, since `main` can't be a coroutine. A real `Task` also keeps an exception from `unhandled_exception`, through `std::current_exception()`, and rethrows it in `await_resume`, the way a `std::future` does in *Tasks, Futures, and a Thread Pool*, in the Concurrency section.

example.code:
// -O2 -std=c++20 -fcoroutines
#include <coroutine>
#include <cstdint>
#include <exception>
#include <iostream>
#include <utility>

template <typename T>
class Task {
public:
    struct promise_type {
        T value{};
        std::coroutine_handle<> continuation;  // the coroutine that awaits this one
        Task get_return_object() { return Task{std::coroutine_handle<promise_type>::from_promise(*this)}; }
        std::suspend_always initial_suspend() noexcept { return {}; }  // lazy: start when awaited
        struct FinalAwaiter {
            bool await_ready() noexcept { return false; }
            std::coroutine_handle<> await_suspend(std::coroutine_handle<promise_type> me) noexcept {
                return me.promise().continuation;  // symmetric transfer: jump back to the awaiter
            }
            void await_resume() noexcept {}
        };
        FinalAwaiter final_suspend() noexcept { return {}; }
        void return_value(T v) { value = std::move(v); }
        void unhandled_exception() { std::terminate(); }
    };

    explicit Task(std::coroutine_handle<promise_type> h) : h_(h) {}
    Task(Task&& other) noexcept : h_(std::exchange(other.h_, {})) {}
    ~Task() {
        if (h_) h_.destroy();
    }

    bool await_ready() const noexcept { return false; }
    std::coroutine_handle<> await_suspend(std::coroutine_handle<> awaiter) noexcept {
        h_.promise().continuation = awaiter;
        return h_;  // symmetric transfer: start this task now, in place of the awaiter
    }
    T await_resume() { return std::move(h_.promise().value); }

    T run() {  // for main, which isn't a coroutine: start the task and read its result
        h_.promise().continuation = std::noop_coroutine();
        h_.resume();
        return std::move(h_.promise().value);
    }

private:
    std::coroutine_handle<promise_type> h_;
};

Task<std::int64_t> mid(std::int64_t bid, std::int64_t ask) {
    co_return (bid + ask) / 2;
}

Task<std::int64_t> position_value(std::int64_t qty, std::int64_t bid, std::int64_t ask) {
    const std::int64_t m = co_await mid(bid, ask);  // runs mid to its co_return, then comes back
    co_return qty * m;
}

Task<std::int64_t> book_value() {
    std::int64_t total = 0;
    total += co_await position_value(100, 19274, 19276);
    total += co_await position_value(-50, 41008, 41012);
    co_return total;
}

Task<std::int64_t> replay(std::int64_t n) {  // a million awaits that each finish at once
    std::int64_t sum = 0;
    for (std::int64_t i = 1; i <= n; ++i) sum += co_await mid(i, i);
    co_return sum;
}

int main() {
    std::cout << "book value " << book_value().run() << '\n';
    std::cout << "a million awaits, sum " << replay(1000000).run() << '\n';
}

